#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064078 :question/id #uuid "115b3fb1-1041-532a-b5fa-4d8a53c9ab2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-450" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x},$ writing your final answer as a single function." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=1+2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C = \\dfrac 1 2\\ln K$ for another arbitrary constant $K > 0,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K\\cdot |1+2x|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1+2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x  = \\dfrac 1 2 \\ln \\left(K |1+2x|\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad00ef9a-c72e-529b-892d-4e941a28ae9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (K \\mid 2x + 5 \\mid)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063277} {:answer/id #uuid "c964e442-f55e-568e-93e5-09a4616d3a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\ln (K \\